1 NOTE ES.41091.AGI/1.16404.11.3.18//CONTRATACION,5767
2 CONT Entre Privilegios de juros sobre la Contratacion
2 CONT 1560-1593.
2 CONT Please read the other names contained within the list that places Brigida Maldonado in Santo Domingo and perhaps, Isla de San Juan, Pedro Sepulveda, Isabel de Torres, and finally Leonor Ponce de Leon. There are so very few documen
2 CONC ts out there in regard to Dona Leonor Ponce de Leon that I have been informed that many people doubt her existence. I believe that she wasn't the daughter of an inkeeper as some people have assumed. I believe that she was a lady w
2 CONC ith education and title that were afforded to women during her day. Here she is petitioning before la Contratacion probably for the bienes that Juan Ponce de Leon left after his death. The year was 1573, 52 years after her husband
2 CONC 's death. She was still living in Santo Domingo. Or, it might even be her daughter? The possibilities are open for discussion.
2 CONT Ramo 1. De Teresa Medina
2 CONT Ramo 2. De Brigida Maldonado
2 CONT Ramo 7. De Pedro Sepulveda
2 CONT Ano 1573
2 CONT Ramo 7. De Dona Leonor Ponce de Leon
2 CONT Ano 1573:
2 CONT  De Isabel de Torres.
2 CONT I am connecting what I feel are Leonor Ponce de Leon's parents. According to the partial truth contained within the legend surrounding her, she was Juan Ponce de Leon's cousin.
2 CONT Where people came up with that "her father was an inn keeper". This is an incredible and preposterous story. On;y time will tell if my theory is correct or not. The time sequence and period are perfect for my connection of Leonor 
2 CONC Ponce de Leon Guzman to Ines Ponce de Leon and Luis de Guzman.
2 CONT This one is Copyright 2008 John J. browne Ayes, All Rights Reserved, National and International.
2 CONT Under common Law Copyright and Intellectual Copyright Laws. No one may draw information from this family group without my permission. If any one uses this information within a book or manuscript of any type John J. Browne Ayes is 
2 CONC the sole owner of this copyright and must be advised and any proceeds from manuscript, book, etc must be shared with him.
2 CONT Research Copyright 2008, John J. Browne Ayes
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From: Miriam Maldonado Sun. April 20, 2008  "John,  I just put a book down to read the e-mail. The book is about Juan Ponce de Leon. According to historians, Juan married a relative , Leonor. She was a Castilian, daughter of an in
2 CONC n keeper in Santo Domingo. Their children were: Juana, Leonor, Isabel and Luis. The issue of whether she was an Indian or not appears when he became a widower. According to Murga Sanz and Devereux, historians, Juan P.de Leon marri
2 CONC ed Juana de Pineda in Seville late 1517 or early 1518.  Murga and Devereux are the only ones with the theory that Leonor must have been an Indian because her name does not appear in any document.  Yet their story of Juan P.de Leon
2 CONC  marrying Pineda in the time they stated is not accurate. When Juan returned to Puerto Rico from Seville in May, 1518, Leonor was still alive. This fact was repeated by his children and grandchildren in stories told.   It could be
2 CONC  that he had illegitimate children from an Indian, or if I remember well, one Indian when baptized, took Juan Ponce de Leon's name. I can't recall if the Indian was baptized in P.R. or Santo Domingo ( at the Higuey where Juan live
2 CONC d before coming to P.R. ).  In some cases the baptized took the name and surname of the godfather. This also happened with some Jews when they converted to Christianity during the Inquisition. They knew they would suffer discrimin
2 CONC ation even baptized, so by changing their names they thought they would be spared. There is a term in psychology that is used pertaining a situation when the oppressed identifies with the oppressor for survival and sanity. It's a 
2 CONC syndrome (I'm having a senior moment and can't think of the word). There was a Taino ( Lucayan )who traveled with Christopher Columbus in subsequent voyages after 1492. When he got baptized he took the name of Diego Colon.   If I 
2 CONC find more information on this matter, I'll send it. The book I'm reading (for the second time) is :Juan Ponce de Leon and the Spanish Discovery of Puerto Rico and Florida ". The author is Robert H. Fuson, The McDonald & Woodward P
2 CONC ublishing Co, Blacksburg - Virginia, 2000. The book is fascinating. It has a lot of history: Spain, Puerto Rico, Santo Domingo, the voyages, the Conquest, the Indians,  etc. It has also some of Juan Ponce de Leon's genealogy."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Miriam, John,
2 CONT Perhaps this might be of interest-- David Cuesta Camacho & Adolfo Perez Comas have published an article in the recent issue of Hereditas 2007 v.8 no. 2, p.37-105, "Juan Ponce de Leon el Adelantado, Su entorno familiar y algunas pr
2 CONC oyecciones familiares en PR." They are working on her origins, and they think that they were related, since both came from the same place, Santervas de Campos, secondly, because they married as soon as she got to Hispanola, and th
2 CONC irdly, at that time there were so few people that those of noble lineage preferred to marry among themselves. [p.39]
2 CONT They do mention both the Murga Sanz and the Devereux books, along with many documents from the Archivo General de Indias, Libros parroquiales and other sources.
2 CONT Saludos,"
2 CONT Ellen.
