1 NOTE {geni:about_me} After the death of Henry IV (1474), the traditional Castilian stronghold of the late monarch, Segovia, was placed under the guardianship of Andrés de Cabrera and Beatriz de Bobadilla, which can give an idea of the 
2 CONC degree of confidence that Ferdinand and Isabel deposited on them.  Keep in mind that the tower of Segovia was one of the major strengths of the time and also had in their warehouses with the treasure of the crown of Castile, ie al
2 CONC l economic reserves of the kingdom, by which custody was a high-risk.  The next, 1475 year, Beatriz itself was responsible for the custody of Segovia, as her husband, after receiving orders of King Ferdinand, commanded one of the 
2 CONC squads besieged Brotherhood Toro to recover from Portuguese rule.  In 1476, however, one of the gentlemen Segovia, Alfonso Maldonado, abducted the father of Dona Beatriz, Pedro de Bobadilla, and threatened to kidnap the Princess M
2 CONC ary, who was, at the time, also in the custody of marriage, if not the Catholic Monarchs gave the warden to their rightful possessors.  Finally, Maldonado withdrew its hostile attitude, although several segovianos knights, led by 
2 CONC Bishop Juan Arias and notable Table and Pedro Luis Maldonado, this father of Alfonso, disagreed with the government of Dona Beatriz and her husband.  It is quite possible, however, that the enmity of Andrés Segovia was only agains
2 CONC t Cabrera, who had formerly been King Henry Butler;  to demonstrate the value of Beatriz de Bobadilla in front of Segovia, worth the words of G. Fernández de Oviedo (ed. cit, p 266..) 
2 CONT
2 CONT  "The Marchioness of Moya, who was Dona Beatriz de Bobadilla, was to Segovia and threw the çibdad [... illegible] and reforço the alcáçar, and his servants with him and servers became a free mine and took its possession." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Toledo, although little is known of them between 1480 and 1492, except Dona Beatriz was part of the retinue of the queen in making Baza (1489), where her husband also fought.  Thanks to that territorial increase, were able to foun
2 CONC d two primogeniture to his sons: Juan Cabrera, a successor to the Marquis of Moya, who married Ana de Mendoza, daughter of the Duke of Infantry, Diego Hurtado de Mendoza;  while the second son, Pedro de Bobadilla, reached the titl
2 CONC e of Count of Chinchon 1517 by decree of Emperor Carlos , due to the services provided in the rebellion of the commoners.  A third son, Francisco de Bobadilla, chose an ecclesiastical career and was chaplain of Queen Elizabeth fro
2 CONC m 1503, later going to be Queen Dona Juana .
2 CONT
