1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatriz de la Cueva de Alvarado (c. 1498 – 11 September 1541), nicknamed "La Sinventura" - "the unfortunate" - was a Spanish noblewoman from Úbeda in Andalucia who became the governor of the Spanish colony of Guatemala for a few d
2 CONC ays in September 1541. She was killed by an earthquake shortly after taking office. Unique as the only woman to hold such a position in a major division of Spanish Latin America in colonial times, she is credited with having intro
2 CONC duced the Spanish style of house construction and Spanish customs into Guatemala. She was buried in the cathedral of Santiago de los Caballeros de Guatemala.  Beatriz de la Cueva was born to aristocratic parents, probably in 1498[
2 CONC 1] or 1500,[2] in Úbeda, Jaén province, Andalusia, Spain.[2] Her father was Luis de la Cueva, admiral of Santo Domingo and comendador of Alcántara. She was descended from Beltrán de la Cueva, 1st Duke of Alburquerque; her paternal
2 CONC  uncle was the Duke of Alburquerque. Her mother was Maria Manrique de Benavides. Most of her brothers died in battle in Algiers. Her sister Francisca was a maid of honour to King Charles I's sister Eleanor[3] and wife of the conqu
2 CONC istador Pedro de Alvarado.[1] After Francisca died, her former husband Alvarado returned to Spain and married Beatriz on 17 October 1538. The marriage required papal authorisation because of their affinity. Another of Beatriz' unc
2 CONC les was Francisco de los Cobos, the personal secretary of King Charles I. He persuaded the king to intercede with the pope on the couple's behalf, and gave 1500 gold pesos as the bride's dowry.[4]  Most of what little is known of 
2 CONC Beatriz de la Cueva relates to Alvarado's second voyage to Spain, and her own sudden death.[3] The couple returned to Guatemala by 1539, and her large retinue included 20 maids of honour.[5] As Alvarado lay dying on a battlefield 
2 CONC in Mexico in June 1541, he bequeathed both his wealth and his right to rule to Beatriz. Messengers carried the news from Mexico to Guatemala. Beatriz received them on 29 August and, distraught, ordered all her palace's internal an
2 CONC d external walls painted black.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatriz de la Cueva then successfully promoted herself as her husband's successor as governor of Guatemala among Alvarado's colleagues and friends.[7] Although Alvarado had left her brother Francisco as acting governor; she summon
2 CONC ed him, together with bishop Francisco Marroquín,[8] and various other officials to her palace. Claiming that the royal treasurer aspired to Alvarado's title as governor and captain-general, Beatriz demanded that they make her gov
2 CONC ernor instead.[6] After heated discussion, Beatriz was declared governor the next morning, 9 September 1541.[9] Beatriz de la Cueva signed the documents as la sin ventura, "the unfortunate", referring to the recent loss of her hus
2 CONC band.[10] She soon appointed her brother Francisco as her lieutenant, entrusted with day-to-day matters, while retaining for herself all powers relating to the distribution of wealth, Indian labour, and encomiendas.[9] Thus, she b
2 CONC ecame the first female governor in the New World,[1] although her cousin María de Toledo had previously served as vicereine of Santo Domingo.[11] In fact, the royal treasurer and his supporters continued to plot against her. When 
2 CONC spies informed the newly-appointed governor, she ordered her brother to seize the plotters. The conspirators had planned to seize power in a coup early in the morning of September 11, and evaded the soldiers sent to arrest them by
2 CONC  hiding in an abandoned house.[12]  Meanwhile, torrential rains combined with an earthquake led to the collapse of the crater wall of the Volcán de Agua in the early hours of 11 September. Floodwaters from the former crater lake r
2 CONC ushed downhill along what had been a deep arroyo toward the colonial capital of Santiago de los Caballeros de Guatemala in the valley below, carving a huge scar down the mountainside.[9] The three-storey stone palace had been buil
2 CONC t beside the arroyo, high on the volcano's slope, overlooking the fledgling colonial capital. Beatriz grabbed her five-year-old daughter Anica and fled to her rooftop chapel. The wall of water swept away some of the Indian servant
2 CONC s, and Beatriz' stepdaughter Leonor who had run downstairs with other children. Leonor survived the disaster by clinging to a tree, but the other children all died.[13] While all this was happening, the plotters were attempting to
2 CONC  storm the palace, defended by Francisco and his soldiers. All were swept to their deaths. Beatriz and Anica died when a second tremor struck, and the roof of the chapel collapsed on top of them.[14] Beatriz de la Cueva was buried
2 CONC  in the cathedral of the rebuilt Santiago de los Caballeros de Guatemala at the insistence of the bishop in spite of local opposition, and Leonor had Pedro de Alvarado reburied at her side in 1580.[14]  Aside from briefly being th
2 CONC e first female colonial governor in the New World, Beatriz de la Cueva is credited with introducing Spanish customs and the Spanish house style into Guatemala.[14] In the aftermath of her death Beatriz de la Cueca was blamed for t
2 CONC he disaster as it was considered to be a godly punishment for her sins. Additionally her fate became a cautionary tale about the positioning of a women in political positions.[15] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Maura 1993, p. 905. Vega 2003, p. 140. Vega 2003, p. 139. Gutiérrez Escudero 1991, p. 106. Adams 1995, p. 39. Adams 1995, p. 40. Recinos 1952, 1986, pp. 214–215. Adams 1995, p. 40. Myers 2004, pp. 213–214. Adams 1995, p. 41. Gutié
2 CONC rrez Escudero 1991, p. 116. Vega 2003, p. 35. Adams 1995, pp. 41–42. Adams 1995, p. 42. Adams 1995, p. 43. Petit-Breuilh 2004, pp. 75–76.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Adams, Jerome R. (1995). Notable Latin American Women: Twenty-nine Leaders, Rebels, Poets, Battlers, and Spies, 1500–1900. Jefferson, North Carolina, US and London, UK: McFarland. ISBN 0-7864-0022-6. OCLC 31328416.
2 CONT     Gutiérrez Escudero, Antonio (1991). Pedro de Alvarado: el conquistador del país de los quetzales (in Spanish). Mexico City, Mexico: Red Editorial Iberoamericana. ISBN 968-456-128-8. OCLC 29629609
2 CONT     Matthew, Laura E. (2012). Memories of Conquest: Becoming Mexicano in Colonial Guatemala (hardback). First Peoples. Chapel Hill, North Carolina, USA: University of North Carolina Press. ISBN 978-0-8078-3537-1. OCLC 752286995.
2 CONT     Maura, Juan Francisco (December 1993). "En busca de la verdad: Algunas mujeres excepcionales de la conquista" (in Spanish). Hispania. Walled Lake, Michigan, US: American Association of Teachers of Spanish and Portuguese. Vol. 
2 CONC 76, No. 4, pp. 904–910. JSTOR 343929 (subscription required)
2 CONT     Myers, Paul A. (2004). North to California: The Spanish Voyages of Discovery, 1533–1603. Coral Springs, Florida, US: Llumina Press. ISBN 1-59526-251-2. OCLC 54929617
2 CONT     Petit-Breuilh Sepúlveda, María Eugenia (2004). "Análisis de las erupciones en hispanoamérica durante los últimos cinco siglos". La historia eruptiva de los volcanes hispanoamericanos (Siglos XVI al XX): El modelo chileno (in S
2 CONC panish). Huelva, Spain: Casa de los volcanes. ISBN 84-95938-32-4.
2 CONT     Recinos, Adrian (1986) [1952]. Pedro de Alvarado: Conquistador de México y Guatemala (in Spanish) (2nd ed.). Guatemala: CENALTEX Centro Nacional de Libros de Texto y Material Didáctico "José de Pineda Ibarra". OCLC 243309954.
2 CONT     Vega, Carlos B. (2003). Conquistadoras: Mujeres Heroicas de la Conquista de America (in Spanish). Jefferson, North Carolina, US and London, UK: McFarland. ISBN 0-7864-1601-7. OCLC 52799815.
2 CONT
2 CONT FURTHER READING: Maura, Juan Francisco (December 2005). Españolas de Ultramar. Colección Parnaseo-Lemir. Valencia: Publicaciones de la Universitat de València, 2005, 300pp.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro de Alvarado se había casado en primeras nupcias con Francisca de la Cueva, sobrina del influyente duque de Alburquerque, pero ésta murió nada más desembarcar en Veracruz y, gracias a una dispensa papal, pudo casarse con su h
2 CONC ermana Beatriz. 
2 CONT
2 CONT No sin razón, ésta ha pasado a la historia como la “sinventura”. Su matrimonio apenas duró un año, por eso pasó a la historia como la "sin ventura"
2 CONT
2 CONT Gobernadora de Guatemala de julio a septiembre de 1541
2 CONT
