1 NOTE {geni:about_me} House of Lasso de la Vega From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia Coat of Arms of the House of Lasso de la Vega (against a gold field with "Ave Maria Gratia Plena" written in blue) combined with the Arms of the House
2 CONC  of Mendoza.
2 CONT
2 CONT The House de la Vega, Laso de la Vega or Lasso de la Vega (sometimes even Garci Lasso de la Vega or Garcilaso de la Vega depending on the family member referenced) was a Spanish noble line from the Kingdom of Castile. The family o
2 CONC rigins lie in the areas now known as Torrelavega which was established in the Middle Ages. The House of Lasso de la Vega was one of the most important families in the territory which now makes up Cantabria and they dominated a lar
2 CONC ge amount of the terrain and property between the Torre de la Vega and the Castillo de Argüeso.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT This noble house merged with the House of Mendoza which subsequently adopted its motto, "Ave Maria, Gratia Plena", after the marriage of Leonor Lasso de la Vega, the daughter and only heir of Garci Lasso Ruiz de la Vega, and Diego
2 CONC  Hurtado de Mendoza, the Head Admiral of Castile. From this marriage was born Íñigo López de Mendoza, the first Marqios of Santillana. After this union, the lordship the House of Lasso de la Vega was passed on as property of his s
2 CONC uccessors, the Dukes of the Infantado.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family reached the height of its power in the 15th Century when facing off against the monarchy. They were able to set up an system of Mayordomazgos independent of the crown and based in the Castillo de Pedraja which belonged 
2 CONC to the family. During this time, there were a great many power struggles going on in the area of Cantabria, creating a number of social conflicts.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT After the merger with the House of Mendoza, the Cantabrian surname "Lasso de la Vega" was passed on through the maternal line at later times throughout the years and is associated with various soldiers, poets, and Golden Age write
2 CONC rs such as Garcilaso de la Vega, the soldier and poet, and Inca Garcilaso, the historian from the Viceroyalty of Peru.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Gobernador interino de la isla de Cuba.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 5543. Elvira Lasso de la Vega (Source: (1) Alberto Ferrer-Vallaint (Bayamo y Puerto Príncipe), Carlos J. Zerquera (Trinidad y A.G.I), y Juan R. Peix (Sancti-Spíritus y La Habana), "Descendencia de Vasco Porcallo hasta la sexta gen
2 CONC eración," 1963-1984, p. 35., (2) Carlos J. Zerquera y Fernández de Lara, "Rojas," papeles inéditos, Rojas de Manuel de Rojas y Córdoba.). She was the daughter of 11086. Vasco (i) Porcallo de Figueroa.
2 CONT
2 CONT Most of the information on this branch comes from a Message board of Eveleo de las Mercedes Astray Caneda y del Junco at https://www.ancestry.com/boards/localities.caribbean.cuba.general/6110.1/mb.ashx
