1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Shortly before his death in 1254, Pope Innocent IV established the Inquisition, an organization that from its beginnings until the Enlightenment was responsible for the torture and death of many thousands. As the I
2 CONC nquisition spread its power, conversions everywhere drew increasingly large numbers. However, those Jews who embraced the cross—the conversos, or New Christians—and who had chosen to stay on the Iberian Peninsula remained under co
2 CONC nstant suspicion and had to fear secret denunciation and the omnipresent spies of the Holy Office.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4In 1449, during the rule of João of Castile, the first outbreak of hostility was directed against the New Christians of Toledo, primarily because some of them had achieved high positions at the royal court. Among them was Don Alv
2 CONC aro de Luna, a financial advisor to the king. He was executed a few years later.
2 CONT
