1 NOTE {geni:about_me} This ancestor is my first choice of where to centralize my identity in regards to European Ancestry. Reason being that she and her immediate plus extended family escorts represents the earliest and youngest ancesto
2 CONC r to reach the new world circa 1523,  a mere 30 years after the first ships with Iberian Peninsula European explorers. The continued ships after the first exploration were full of people with intent to colonize in the name of Span
2 CONC ish Royalty, Government and Religion.  Her family composite is a very interesting and eclectic result of people from Northern Spain and rest of Europe but also very strong in Hebrew Ancestry. When she married Francisco Vasquez de 
2 CONC Coronado y Lujan and they together explored North to include most of modern day Arizona, New Mexico and parts of Colorado, Kansas and Oklahoma. Also very interesting to me is that at least two of the people in her family group arr
2 CONC iving in 1523 were of the family name de Torres. There is an explorer by name of Luis de la Torre, a converso, who was the interpretor on the original Santa Maria crew, got left behind intentionaly by Colon but somehow got lost in
2 CONC  the transition records with suspicions that he had been a traitor to the cause and helped the indiginous  but then reappears in same name with the court with Hernan Cortes in being governance over the taking of Aztec lands and es
2 CONC courts of Coronado as a relative of his wife and my first ancestor choice, Beatriz de Estrada well after the organization of the explorations north from Mexico City in 1540 through 1544.
2 CONT
2 CONT Name
2 CONT Beatriz Estrada de la Caballeria
2 CONT Father
2 CONT Mother
2 CONT Misc. Notes
2 CONT Alonso de Estrada (1470-1530)
2 CONT Marina Gutierrez Flores de la Caballería (-1530)
2 CONT The "pruebas de limpieza" documents for don Jorge de Alvarado y Villafaña provide somewhat fragmentary genealogical
2 CONT information about the Gutiérrez de la Caballería family. However, when this material is combined with additional
2 CONT information extracted from records of the Spanish Inquisition of Ciudad Real (1483-1485), we can reconstruct a lineage
2 CONT from a Jewish-converso family of the late 1300s, a family known to have practiced Jewish customs and honored Jewish
2 CONT ceremonies after conversion to Catholicism, to the numerous members of one of the most influential and politically
2 CONT powerful extended families of sixteenth century Nueva España, including, doña Beatriz de Estrada, wife of don Francisco
2 CONT Vásquez de Coronado.
2 CONT Doña Beatriz de Estrada and Her Family
2 CONT Doña Beatriz de Estrada was a daughter of don Alonso de Estrada and doña Marina Flores Gutiérrez de la Caballería.
2 CONT Having served the royal crown faithfully in Flanders (Netherlands) and Sicily, don Alonso de Estrada (b.ca. 1470, Ciudad
2 CONT Real, Castilla la Nueva) found himself in the favor of the Catholic Monarchs of Spain, Rey don Fernando II, El Católico, of
2 CONT Aragón and Reina doña Isabel, La Católica, of Castilla (r.1479-1504). While in service as Corregidor of the city of
2 CONT Cáceres, Estrada was given royal appointment in 1523 as Tesorero (Treasurer) de Nueva España. Don Alonso proceeded
2 CONT to Nueva España arriving in Veracruz the same year as his appointment, having left his wife and children in Spain. This
2 CONT ambitious and grandiose man quickly entered into the political intrigue of Nueva España and was eventually successful in
2 CONT supplanting the great conquistador of Tenochtitlán (Mexico City), don Hernán Cortés, as Governor of Nueva España
2 CONT (1526-1528). In early 1528, don Alosno’s wife and their five youngest children, all daughters, arrived in Nueva España
2 CONT having successfully completed the three month journey from Spain in the company of fray Julián Gárces, first bishop of
2 CONT Tlaxcala.
2 CONT Spouses
2 CONT 1
2 CONT Father
2 CONT Mother
2 CONT Francisco Vasquez de Coronado
2 CONT Juan Vasquez de Coronado
2 CONT Isabel de Luja
2 CONT This family also captured the interest of people who were able to research and put into book form a look at the very strong women in that family.  The following few paragraphs are information from that book.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Flint, Shirley Cushing. ''No Mere Shadows: Faces of Widowhood in Early Colonial Mexico''. University of New Mexico Press, Albuquerque, New Mexico, 2013, pages 89-106. … '''Chapter Five: “Doña Beatriz de Estrada Chooses Prayer”''' 
2 CONC … http://books.google.com/books?id=Ks3ZORm9KlMC&pg=PA14&lpg=PA14&dq=alonso+de+estrada&source=bl&ots=QtXmOGy0uh&sig=TGsWtkIv3AyjMDeWh3dhaLpbLX8&hl=en&sa=X&ei=eHtQVNioHZawyASQ_ILACQ&ved=0CEUQ6AEwBjgK#v=onepage&q=alonso%20de%20estrad
2 CONC a&f=false 
2 CONT
2 CONT Beatris was probably 7 years old when she emigrated Spain although she may have not yet been born.  Her mother gave birth to a child shortl after arriving in Vera Cruz and it most likely was for Beatriz....or she arrived In Nueva 
2 CONC Espana in 1523. Her parents are: Don Alfonso de Estrada was the natural son of Ferdinand the Catholic and Doña Luisa de Estrada daughter of Don Fernán, Duke of Aragon. Don Fernan was one of the most illustrious greats of Spain who
2 CONC , among many other things, had been ambassador to England. He also arranged Catherine's marriage to Henry VIII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the conversos de la Caballeria found fame and wealth, they created divisions in the family. Bonafos' son Judah became Christian and took the name of Gaspar, but his daughter Reyna remained Jewish. Much to the grief of the
2 CONC  Jewish community, Vidal Joseph, a learned man who had translated classic works into Spanish and had written Hebrew poems, also adopted Christianity.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pedro de la Cavalleria, a secretary of the royal treasury under King Alfonso V of Aragon, Catalonia, Napoles, y de Sicily (1396-1458), forged an affidavit of "pureza de sangre" to deny any Jewish blood (Castro 549).
2 CONT
2 CONT Mariana de la Cavalleria. a descendant of the de la Cavalleria who became Christians, married Alonso de Estrada, a royal treasurer of the Spanish crown in the 1520s in México.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Alonso de Etrada was a colorful figure who was supposedly the bastard son of King Ferdinand II (1451-1516) of Aragon. Mariana de la Caballeria was a daughter of Juan Gutierrez de la Cavalleria and Mayor Flores de Guevara.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Mariana's grandfather, Mene de la Cavalleria relapsed into Judaism. After he died his bones were burned by the Inquisition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eventually, as the rest of Sephardim who left Spain rather than convert to Christianity, the members of the de la Cavalleria family who remained faithful to Judaism scattered throughout Europe and the Ottoman empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT This family represents a vital link of the important events following the arrival of Spanish Ancestors in Nueva Espana and the development of various territories in what we now know as South Central and North America. the followin
2 CONC g link is larger view of the family from Spain and arriving in the new world.
2 CONT
2 CONT About edit | history:
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Alfonso de Estrada was the natural son of Ferdinand the Catholic and Doña Luisa de Estrada daughter of Don Fernán, Duke of Aragon. Don Fernan was one of the most illustrious greats of Spain who, among many other things, had be
2 CONC en ambassador to England. He also arranged Catherine's marriage to Henry VIII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand and Luisa were unmarried teenagers at the time of their sexual exploration. Shortly after Alfonso's birth, Ferdinand at the tender age of seventeen married his cousin, Isabella of Castile. This marriage was a necessity t
2 CONC o assure the growth and development of the Kingdoms of Castile and Aragon. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In later years, he recognized his son, Don Alfonso and named him Duke of Aragon.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alonso de Estrada was born in Ciudad Real, Spain probably between 1470 and 1480 and died in 1530. He was married to Marina Gutiérrez Flores de la Cavalleria. They had two sons and five daughters. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Luis Alfonso Fernándes de Estrada stayed in Ciudad Real and Juan Alonso became fray Juan de la Magdalena. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Luisa, Marina, Ana, Francisca, and Beatris came to New Spain with their parents in 1523.
2 CONT
2 CONT •Luisa married Captain Jorgue de Alvarado, a conqueror of México and Governor of Guatemala; and a brother of Pedro de Alvarado; their children were Jorgue de Alvarado, Luisa, and Leonor.
2 CONT
2 CONT •Marina married Luis de Saavreda Guzman; their children were Alonso de Saavreda; Juan who married Regina de la Cadena;
2 CONT
2 CONT Ana married New Spain Treasurer Juan Alonso de Sosa; their children were Juan Alonso de Sosa, baptized February 4, 1542, married Marina de Guevara. They had several children, a daughter married Diego de Ayala, son of licenciado Di
2 CONC ego de Ayala, alcalde de la Audencia de México. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ayala's succession is probably the one that produced Leonor de Ayala who married Joseph de Trevño and started a line of Ayalas in Nuevo León. Their children took the Ayala name, rather than the father's name, Treviño.
2 CONT
2 CONT •Francisca married Alonso Avalalos de Saavreda 
2 CONT
2 CONT •Beatris married Francisco Vasquez de Coronado, the discoverer of the Grand Canyon.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''==Alonso de Estrada also had a natural son, Bartolome, with Ana Rodriguez Anhaifa. Bartolome became a secular priest.=='''
2 CONT
2 CONT Estrada is an interesting historical figure because he was probably the only son of a king of Spain who ever lived in México and left a large number of descendants some of whom are now in the US.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT . Historian Peggy K. Liss states that Estrada was a natural son of King Ferdinand (Lizz 167).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Also another historian Thomas Hugh in his book the Conquest: Montezuma, Cortés, and the Fall of Old México, states that it was believed that Alonso de Estrada was a natural son of King Ferdinand (Hugh 574). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In New Spain, de Estrada served as royal treasurer, lieutenant governor, and governor. Historian Francisco Fernandez del Castillo states that he learned from a document from the Inquisition (reference cited as Archivo de Inqisició
2 CONC n, Tomo 365) that it was common knowledge in Ciudad Real and Almagro that Alonso de Estrada was an illegitimate son of King Ferdinand II of Aragon and Castile (Fernandez del Castillo 399).
2 CONT
2 CONT There are some historians who doubted that Alonso de Estrada was an illegitimate son of King Ferdinand . Among those historians is Norberto de Castro y Tosi who states states that Alonso was the legitimate son of a low ranking hia
2 CONC lgo by the name of Juan de Estrada and unknown wife. The problem with Tosi's hypothesis is that he relies on a testimony provided by Mari Hernandez de Hidalgo, a witness, who did not remember the name of Alonso's mother name.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among historians who believe that Alonso de Estrada was an illegitimate son of King Ferdinand, Francisco Fernandez del Castillo from the Academia Mexicana de la Historia asserts that Alonso occupied very important position in Spai
2 CONC n and later Emperor Charles V named Alonso de Estrada the royal treasurer of New Spain (Fernandez del Castillo 398). 
2 CONT
2 CONT It is not difficult to see that it was a link between appointments to high positions of government in Spain and New Spain and birth into high ranking aristocratic families. For example, Gonzalo de Salazar, Rodrigo de Albornoz, Ped
2 CONC ro Almindez Chirinos were all men from powerful Spanish families and also all of them were protégées of Francisco de los Cobos, the powerful personal secretary of Charles V.
2 CONT
2 CONT •Sources: Indice Geobiografico de Cuarenta Mil Pobladores Españoles de America en el Siglo XVI
2 CONT
2 CONT •Fernández, Francisco del Castillo, Memorias de la Academia Mexicana de la Historia: Correspondiente de la Real de Madrid, (México October-December 1942) Tomo I
2 CONT
2 CONT •Liss, Peggy K. México Under Spain, 1521-1556, Society and the Origins of Nationality The University of Chicago Press, 1975. Carranza, Baltasa Dorante de, Sumaria Relación de los Primeros Pobladores de México.
2 CONT
2 CONT •Thomas, Hugh, Conquest: Montezuma, Cortés, and the Fall of Old México. Simon and Schuster, 1993.
2 CONT
2 CONT • Jose Antonio Esquibel: "The Paternal Ancestry of Don Alonso de Estrada, Tesorero y Gobernador de Nueva Espana, and the Revised Genealogy of the Sosa Albornoz Family", Genealogical Journal (Vol. IV, 1998) of the Society of Hispan
2 CONC ic and Ancestral Research, or SHHAR.
2 CONT
2 CONT <https://gobernantesdemexico.fandom.com/wiki/Alonso_de_Estrada>
2 CONT
2 CONT Alonso de Estrada, gobernador pre-virreinal de Nueva España>
2 CONT
2 CONT Don Alfonso de Estrada era hijo natural de Fernando el Católico y de Doña Luisa de Estrada hija de Don Fernán, Duque de Aragón. Don Fernan fue uno de los más ilustres grandes de España que, entre otras muchas cosas, había sido emb
2 CONC ajador en Inglaterra. También arregló el matrimonio de Catalina con Enrique VIII.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ferdinand y Luisa eran adolescentes solteros en el momento de su exploración sexual. Poco después del nacimiento de Alfonso, Fernando a la tierna edad de diecisiete años se casó con su prima, Isabel de Castilla. En años posteriore
2 CONC s, reconoció a su hijo, don Alfonso y lo nombró duque de Aragón.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alonso de Estrada nació en Ciudad Real, España probablemente entre 1470 y 1480 y murió en 1530. Estuvo casado con Marina Gutiérrez Flores de la Cavalleria. Tuvieron dos hijos y cinco hijas. Luis Alfonso Fernándes de Estrada se que
2 CONC dó en Ciudad Real y Juan Alonso se convirtió en el hermano Juan de la Magdalena. Luisa, Marina, Ana, Francisca y Beatris llegaron a Nueva España con sus padres en 1523.
2 CONT
2 CONT Luisa se casó con el capitán Jorgue de Alvarado, conquistador de México y gobernador de Guatemala; y un hermano de Pedro de Alvarado; sus hijos fueron Jorgue de Alvarado, Luisa y Leonor.
2 CONT
2 CONT •Marina se casó con Luis de Saavreda Guzmán; sus hijos fueron Alonso de Saavreda; Juan que se casó con Regina de la Cadena;
2 CONT
2 CONT •Ana se casó con el Tesorero de Nueva España Juan Alonso de Sosa; sus hijos fueron Juan Alonso de Sosa, bautizado el 4 de febrero de 1542, casado con Marina de Guevara. Tuvieron varios hijos, una hija se casó con Diego de Ayala, h
2 CONC ijo del licenciado Diego de Ayala, alcalde de la Audencia de México. La sucesión de los Ayala es probablemente la que produjo a Leonor de Ayala quien se casó con José de Trevño y comenzó una línea de Ayalas en Nuevo León. Sus hijo
2 CONC s tomaron el nombre de Ayala, en lugar del nombre del padre, Treviño.
2 CONT
2 CONT •Francisca se casó con Alonso Avalalos de Saavreda •Beatris se casó con Francisco Vásquez de Coronado, el descubridor del Gran Cañón.
2 CONT
2 CONT Alonso de Estrada también tuvo un hijo natural, Bartolomé, con Ana Rodríguez Anhaifa. Bartolomé se convirtió en un sacerdote secular.
2 CONT
2 CONT Estrada es una figura histórica interesante porque probablemente fue el único hijo de un rey de España que alguna vez vivió en México y dejó un gran número de descendientes, algunos de los cuales ahora están en los Estados Unidos.
2 CONC  La historiadora Peggy K. Liss afirma que Estrada era un hijo natural del rey Fernando (Lizz 167). También otro historiador Thomas Hugh en su libro La conquista: Moctezuma, Cortés y la caída del Viejo México, afirma que se creía q
2 CONC ue Alonso de Estrada era un hijo natural del rey Fernando (Hugo 574). En la Nueva España, de Estrada sirvió como tesorero real, teniente gobernador y gobernador. El historiador Francisco Fernández del Castillo afirma que aprendió 
2 CONC de un documento de la Inquisición (referencia citada como Archivo de Inqisición, Tomo 365) que era de conocimiento común en Ciudad Real y Almagro que Alonso de Estrada era un hijo ilegítimo del rey Fernando II de Aragón y Castilla
2 CONC  (Fernández del Castillo 399).
2 CONT
2 CONT Hay algunos historiadores que dudaban de que Alonso de Estrada fuera un hijo ilegítimo del rey Fernando. Entre esos historiadores se encuentra Norberto de Castro y Tosi quien afirma que Alonso era el hijo legítimo de un hialgo de 
2 CONC bajo rango llamado Juan de Estrada y esposa desconocida. El problema con la hipótesis de Tosi es que se basa en un testimonio proporcionado por Mari Hernández de Hidalgo, una testigo, que no recordaba el nombre de la madre de Alon
2 CONC so.
2 CONT
2 CONT Entre los historiadores que creen que Alonso de Estrada era un hijo ilegítimo del rey Fernando, Francisco Fernández del Castillo de la Academia Mexicana de la Historia afirma que Alonso ocupó una posición muy importante en España 
2 CONC y más tarde el emperador Carlos V nombró a Alonso de Estrada tesorero real de la Nueva España (Fernández del Castillo 398). No es difícil ver que era un vínculo entre los nombramientos en altos cargos de gobierno en España y la Nu
2 CONC eva España y el nacimiento en familias aristocráticas de alto rango. Por ejemplo, Gonzalo de Salazar, Rodrigo de Albornoz, Pedro Almindez Chirinos eran todos hombres de poderosas familias españolas y también todos eran protegidos 
2 CONC de Francisco de los Cobos, el poderoso secretario personal de 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT  Luis de la Torre: Position in Estrada’s Network
2 CONT 🌱 Status in 1523
2 CONT By 1523, Luis de la Torre is present in New Spain and already part of the royal treasury and administrative cohort, though not yet in a governing role. His responsibilities were tied to oversight of royal revenues and logistical a
2 CONC dministration—functions that naturally placed him in Estrada’s orbit.
2 CONT He is not as visible in the documentation as Salazar, Albornoz, or Zuazo, but he appears in the same bureaucratic ecosystem and is consistently grouped with treasury officials in later testimonies.
2 CONT 🧭 Rise to Co-Governor
2 CONT His prominence increases sharply in:
2 CONT • 	1527–1528, when he serves as co‑governor of New Spain alongside:
2 CONT • 	Alonso de Estrada
2 CONT • 	Gonzalo de Sandoval
2 CONT • 	Rodrigo de Albornoz
2 CONT This governing junta is formed after the disastrous Salazar–Chirino regime and before the arrival of the First Audiencia.
2 CONT Luis de la Torre’s inclusion signals:
2 CONT • 	Crown trust in his administrative reliability
2 CONT • 	His alignment with Estrada’s faction rather than the Salazar–Chirino faction
2 CONT • 	His integration into the treasury‑administrative network that Estrada dominated
2 CONT 🧬 Why He Matters in Estrada’s Network
2 CONT Even though he is “lesser‑known,” he is valuable for your genealogical‑political mapping because:
2 CONT • 	He represents the second tier of royal bureaucrats who supported Estrada’s authority.
2 CONT • 	His later co-governance shows he was already embedded in Estrada’s administrative circle by 1523.
2 CONT • 	His presence helps explain how Estrada maintained institutional continuity during Cortés’s absences.
2 CONT 🗺️ Placement in Your 1523 Network Map
2 CONT He belongs in the “Royal Treasury & Administrative Cohort”, but with a note:
2 CONT • 	1523: Administrative associate, not yet a major political actor
2 CONT • 	1527–1528: Elevated to co-governor, fully aligned with Estrada’s faction
