1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Pedro de Valdés Menéndez-Lavandera
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT Valdés Menéndez-Lavandera, Pedro de. Roces, Gijón (Asturias), 1544 – Asturias, pts xvii. Governor and captain general of the island of Cuba, captain general of the Navy, admiral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Juan de Valdés de Villar el Mozo , councilor of the Gijón City Council, and Teresa Menéndez Lavandera. Pedro de Valdés was not a man called to the tranquility of study, but rather a man of eventful life, a soldier and a sai
2 CONC lor with a stormy life, who barely began his studies in literature, since, before he was sixteen years old, he was already sailing, most likely with Pedro. Menéndez de Avilés, the advance, his first school of sea and war.
2 CONT
2 CONT From a Memorial that he addressed to Philip III, it is known that Pedro de Valdés, at the age of sixteen, went to Naples in 1560 as an entertainer for Viceroy Parafán. He soon began to serve in galleys, where for a war feat he was
2 CONC  granted, as recorded in a new petition from his son Fernando Valdés, the habit of the Military Order of Santiago and the command of Oreja. Before he had been named a gentleman by the mouth of her Majesty.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1565 he left Naples and moved to Cádiz, where he embarked with the squadron of the advance Pedro Menéndez de Avilés, his future father-in-law, to the conquest of La Florida. On March 24 of the same year, 1565, the deed of the m
2 CONC arriage capitulations of Pedro de Valdés with the advance's daughter, Ana Menéndez de Avilés, was signed. The signature was made between the parents of the couple, with Diego Valdés also signing, abbot of Cenero who had been a wai
2 CONC ter to the archbishop of Seville, Fernando Valdés Salas, famous for the inquisitorial process against Bartolomé de Carranza. The marriage of Pedro de Valdés and Ana Menéndez de Avilés barely lasted two months. Her parents did not 
2 CONC even have time to make the bond of eldership effective in favor of her daughter Ana, since she was murdered by order of Juan de Valdés el Mozo , due to rumors that she had stained her honor. of the family. Murder very felt in Gijó
2 CONC n and whose process disappeared from the official archives, although a copy may probably exist in a family archive. The event occurred at the end of the year 1568, when the advance and Pedro de Valdés were in La Florida and could 
2 CONC do nothing for their relatives, because when they came to the Court, one to ask for justice and the other to request mercy, Philip II was ruthless with Juan de Valdés and ordered him to be beheaded, while he banished his wife Tere
2 CONC sa Menéndez Lavandera from Gijón.
2 CONT
2 CONT Once the conquest of Florida was over, the advance and Pedro de Valdés, the latter without royal permission, returned to Spain. In the same year of 1570 there is a certification of the advance signed in Madrid, in favor of Pedro d
2 CONC e Valdés. These were years in which Pedro de Valdés dedicated himself, by order of the King, to recruiting companies for Naples as Commissioner of Infantry and to building ships for the King in the Cantabrian Sea, until in 1575 he
2 CONC  was named captain general of the Navy of West and Coasts of Andalusia.
2 CONT
2 CONT On February 10, 1574, the King granted the advance, for the tenth time, the appointment of a powerful Navy that met in Santander, to quell the conflicts in Flanders. He could not command said fleet because he died in port on Septe
2 CONC mber 17, 1574.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1575, in the month of September, after the death of the advance guard, Pedro Valdés was named captain general of a new squadron. Mostly formed with the same Western Navy, it took Juan Martínez de Recalde to Flanders as
2 CONC  admiral and left him there (actually in the English Channel itself, which Recalde complained about) with all the necessary supplies, in pataches, zabras, pinnaces, etc., and he returned with the large ships, again to the coasts o
2 CONC f Andalusia. Although the King knew perfectly well that without naval support and, of course, without money it was practically impossible to maintain the Netherlands. Immediately Felipe II appointed him again commissioner of Infan
2 CONC try for the recruitment of troops that were to be embarked for Italy. At the beginning of 1580, historical events and the will of Philip II ensured that Portugal passed to the Spanish Crown and that the Cortes of Tomar elected him
2 CONC  King in 1581. Only the prior Antonio de Ocrato and the Terceira island of the Azores did not surrender. before the Spanish king, supported as they were by France and to a certain extent by England as well. Given the turn that thi
2 CONC ngs were taking, and the reports that the King had (Philip II's secret services were numerous and the best organized in Europe) of the French pretensions, he organized, under the command of Valdés, the Navy of the islands of Bayon
2 CONC ne, with the mission of monitoring and patrolling the Portuguese coast. Very soon, in June 1580, Philip II informed Pedro de Valdés to place his entire fleet under the orders of the Duke of Alba.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1581 the King ordered an armada of at least eight ships to be prepared to go to the Terceira island of the Azores, with the mission of waiting in its surroundings for the fleets of New Spain, Tierra Firme and the East Indies, t
2 CONC o accompany them to Sanlúcar and this one, convoy it to Lisbon. Thus Pedro Valdés carried out what was ordered, but, while patrolling the coasts of Terceira, waiting for the Atlantic fleets, being a man of impulse rather than refl
2 CONC ection, he launched a landing on the beach of Angra, where the great disaster of the stampede of the oxen that drove the Spaniards to the beach, killing a large number of Pedro Valdés' relatives, who had to abandon the island and 
2 CONC go out to sea. For this disaster he was imprisoned in the castle of Lisbon. Meanwhile, in 1580, a son was born in Madrid to Isabel de Tovar, daughter of Hernando de Tovar of the Tovar lineage of Valladolid, both of whom were singl
2 CONC e, whom they called Fernando, who in time would be the heir to the Valdés estate. Released from the Lisbon prison, due to the influence of the Marquis of Santa Cruz and the Duke of Alba and due to the need for commands for the gre
2 CONC at Navy, he was named admiral of the Andalusian Squadron and incorporated with it into the Invincible Armada.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to two successive boardings with his own ships, the galleon Nuestra Señora del Rosario, commanded by Pedro Valdés, was left without control, which led Sir Drake to watch it all night and at dawn, after strong Spanish resistanc
2 CONC e, he managed to the surrender of Pedro de Valdés, with more or less favorable conditions for the Spanish. After three or four years imprisoned in England in the Drake family home, he was freed in exchange for ransom. Philip II, t
2 CONC aking advantage of his return, ordered him, before returning to Spain, to carry out a study of the coastal defenses of Flanders. Meanwhile, the King had ordered the imprisonment in Burgos of Diego Flórez, Medina Sidonia's first na
2 CONC val advisor, for having convinced him not to entertain the fleet in rescuing Pedro Valdés.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Philip II died, his son Philip III appointed him, in the year 1600, governor and captain general of the island of Cuba, until he was removed after a residency trial, of course. He returned to Gijón and after trying a new ser
2 CONC vice for King Philip III at Court, he did not succeed, retiring again to his ancestral home, where after five years, three of them blind and sick, he died in the first third of the year. 17th century, having served fifty years und
2 CONC er Philip II and his son Philip III.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources and bibliography: Family Archive of the Count of Revillagigedo; Archives of the City Councils of Valladolid, Oviedo, Avilés and Gijón.
2 CONT
