1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was the son of Fernando de Cabrera y Bobadilla , 1st Count of Chinchón , and Teresa de la Cueva y Álvarez de Toledo, daughter of the Duke of Alburquerque . He married the daughter of the Count of Melito , Mencía
2 CONC  de Mendoza y de la Cerda, and had eight children: four boys and four girls:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Diego Fernández de Cabrera y Bobadilla , who succeeded in the title.
2 CONT # Teresa of Cabrera y la Cueva, married to the Count of Lemos .
2 CONT # Andrés de Cabrera y Bobadilla , bishop of Segovia and Zaragoza .
2 CONT # Pedro de Cabrera y Bobadilla, who fought at La Goleta and Mazalquivir .
2 CONT # Diego de Cabrera y Bobadilla, a student at the Caballeros Manriques school in Alcalá, who died young.
2 CONT # Ana de Cabrera y la Cerda, lady-in-waiting to Queen Anne of Austria .
2 CONT # Mencía de Cabrera y la Cerda, married to the Third Marquis of the Valley of Oaxaca .
2 CONT # Maria Leonor de Cabrera y la Cerda
2 CONT
2 CONT He inherited the extensive county of Chinchón and other properties upon the death of his father Ferdinand in 1521. He also served as steward to Philip II , counselor in the Councils of State , War, Italy and Aragon , perpetual maj
2 CONC or ensign of Segovia - according to the title given in El Bosque on September 20, 1566 - and general treasurer of the Crown of Aragon by hereditary right and royal order of September 17, 1558, given in the monastery of Santa María
2 CONC  de Valldonzella (the office had become vacant due to the death of Rodrigo de Castro Acevedo y Fonseca). 2
2 CONT
2 CONT He died at the Casa del Bosque , in Balsaín (Segovia), on August 19, 1575.
