1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://gw.geneanet.org/antonioalvistur?lang=en&n=valdez+bazan&nz=carballido+rincon&oc=66781&ocz=0&p=fernando+de&pz=epifanio
2 CONT
2 CONT Juan Fernández de Salas y Valdés, que pasó al Perú con Pizarro. Contrajo matrimonio con Usenda de Bazán y Tordoya, natural de Valverde (hija legítima de Gómez de Tordoya y de María de Chaves). De este matrimonio nació a) Fernando 
2 CONC de Valdés y Bazán, quien contrajo matrimonio con Leonor de Tordoya, ambos naturales del Cuzco, en Perú (hija de Gómez de Tordoya y Hojeda, natural del Cuzco, Familiar del Santo Oficio de la Inquisición, y de Mayor Palomino de Ribe
2 CONC ros, natural del Cuzco).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Four Busts House belonged to the spanish family of the Marquis Salas Valdez. It is located in 400 San Agustin Street, and it is decorated with rose windows and columns in relief. The lintel is carved in only one lithic piece a
2 CONC nd we can see four busts and the nobiliary coat of arms. House of conquerors, this house of Plateresque inspiration, is an exceptional testimony of how proud the main families of Cusco were of deriving from the first Spanish inhab
2 CONC itants. This is proved not only by the archaising style of its portico but also by the astonishing use of four stone relieves with some other family portraits. For this reason, the house received the popular name that still preser
2 CONC ves up to our days.
2 CONT
2 CONT The owners of The Four Busts House For a long time, the identity of these characters was forgotten. A detailed documental research performed by the historian Luis Enrique Tord revealed their names at last. They were the conqueror 
2 CONC Juan de Salas and his wife Usenda de Bazan along with his first-born son Fernando de Salas Valdez Bazan and his wife Leonor de Tordoya y Palomino. All of them are wearing clothes according to the time of Felipe II.
